  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    One account stands for all the individual debtor accounts.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The control account and the personal ledger must agree; a difference is a finding.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A credit sale raises the debtor's balance; the other three reduce it.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Returns inwards reduce the debtor's balance; the other two increase it.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    320,000 + 1,450,000 − 1,300,000 − 40,000 − 15,000 = ₦415,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Sales = 250,000 − 180,000 + 500,000 + 20,000 = ₦590,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    200,000 + 800,000 − 750,000 − 30,000 = ₦220,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The debtor owes the money again, so the balance is restored.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Discount allowed reduces what the debtor owes, so it is credited.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The same person's balances in the sales and purchases ledgers are offset.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It summarises the creditors' personal accounts.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It is the bank's record of the firm's account, which is why it differs from the cash book.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    They are already in the cash book but have not reached the bank.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    They are in the cash book but not yet in the statement.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    800,000 + 60,000 − 120,000 = ₦740,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    610,000 − 12,000 − 45,000 + 80,000 = ₦633,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A charge reduces the firm's money at the bank, which is a credit in the cash book.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The bank has received money for the firm, so the cash book is debited.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Money has left the account, so the cash book balance falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The cash book falls and the debtor owes again.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The statement is higher than the truth by those cheques, so they are deducted.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The firm cannot alter the bank's record; it asks the bank to correct it.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    From the bank's point of view the firm's money is a liability, so it appears as a credit.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    A debit balance in an asset account is value held.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Items the bank already knows are posted to the cash book before the two records are compared.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The cheque is written and posted at once but reaches the bank later.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Standing orders are executed by the bank and only then discovered by the firm.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The overdraft grows by the unrecorded charge.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It accounts for every difference and proves the two records agree.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It carries the totals posted from the books of original entry.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Debtors are assets, so the control account normally carries a debit balance.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Creditors are liabilities, so the control account normally carries a credit balance.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    It reduces what the firm owes, so it is debited in that control account.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The customer owes more, so the debtor's balance rises.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The cheque is correct in the cash book; it simply has not reached the bank yet.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The two devices deal with different problems: one with ledger arithmetic, the other with timing.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The drawer had insufficient funds, so the bank refused to pay.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    The book is higher than the statement when cheques have gone out but not yet cleared.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
    Charges are new information about money already spent, so the cash book must record them.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 2.19pt)[
